  // ============================================================
  // ELEMENT: Technetium (Tc, Z=43)
  // THE FIRST ELEMENT IN THIS TABLE WITH NO STABLE ISOTOPES AT ALL -- every prior element's isotopes:: list used enums.DecayMode.STABLE for at least one natural entry; Tc genuinely has none, so both entries below use BETA_MINUS instead. nuclear.properties uses Tc-99's real, well-characterized spin/moment (I=9/2, well-documented via NMR) rather than Tc-98's (the technically-longest-lived isotope, but far less studied) -- a deliberate substitution, flagged rather than silent. abundance is (0,0,0,0) throughout -- not a missing-data placeholder the way it would be for a normal element, but the real physical situation: Tc barely exists in nature (ultra-trace spontaneous-fission product only, ~2.5e-13 g per gram of pitchblende) and this database's abundance fields are about natural terrestrial/cosmic distribution, which for Tc is genuinely near-zero.
  // Several fields (crystal lattice constants, mechanical moduli, thermal properties) are estimates interpolated between neighbors Mo/Ru rather than directly sourced -- flagged throughout, since bulk single-crystal data for a synthetic, intensely radioactive element is genuinely scarce in the literature, not just hard to search for. specific_heat_j_kgk uses a Dulong-Petit estimate (255) rather than one found source's figure (63 J/(kg*K)), which looked internally inconsistent against Tc's own atomic mass. Real, notable fact: Tc's spectral signature has been detected in the atmospheres of some red giant stars, since its longest natural half-life (millions of years) is short on stellar-nucleosynthesis timescales -- direct evidence of ongoing stellar element production.
  // ============================================================

